INT. MILLBROOK TELEPHONE EXCHANGE - NIGHT (1924)

A small room above the feed store. A cord switchboard with a hundred brass jacks. A kerosene lamp backs up a weak electric bulb.

MABEL PRZYBYLSKI, 20s, headset over pinned-up hair, plugs and unplugs cords with practiced speed. She is alone and likes it that way.

A buzz. A light on the board. She plugs in.

MABEL
Number, please.

She listens. Pulls the cord. Connects another. The room breathes.

Boots on the stairs. The door opens without a knock.

A STRANGER in a long coat, hat low, rain on his shoulders. He carries a leather satchel.

MABEL (CONT'D)
We're closed to the public, sir.

STRANGER
I need the line to Harrisburg.

MABEL
That's the long-distance trunk. Company use only.

STRANGER
It's urgent.

MABEL
Everything is, at this hour.

A new buzz. Another light. She ignores it.

STRANGER
My name is Elias Crowe. I'm with the state railway board.

MABEL
You have a paper that says so?

He opens the satchel. Pulls out an envelope. She takes it, reads it by the lamp. Her face tightens.

CROWE
It is in order.

MABEL
It's a telegram form. Anyone can fill one out.

CROWE
Then call the manager.

She hesitates. Pulls a cord. Cranks a handle.

MABEL
(into headset)
Mr. Abernathy? Sorry to wake you. There's a gentleman here.

The earpiece crackles. A thin, irritable voice leaks into the room.

ABERNATHY (V.O.)
At this hour? Is the building on fire?

MABEL
No, sir. A man from the railway board.

ABERNATHY (V.O.)
Tell him to come in the morning.

CROWE
(leaning toward her)
Tell him it concerns the six-ten freight.

Mabel's eyes flick up. She repeats it.

A long silence on the line. Then Abernathy, quieter.

ABERNATHY (V.O.)
Put him through. And Mabel. Plug in the sheriff's line too.

MABEL
Sir?

ABERNATHY (V.O.)
Do it.

She obeys. Her hands are not quite steady now.

The door opens again. TOBY, 12, the messenger boy, cap too big, bursts in dripping and out of breath.

TOBY
Miss Mabel! Somebody cut the wire at the Calder bridge. Pa says the whole east line's dead.

Crowe straightens. Mabel looks from the boy to the stranger.

MABEL
Mr. Crowe. How did you know about the freight?

CROWE
I don't know anything about wires.

TOBY
Then why's your satchel got a coil of copper in it?

Everyone looks. A bright loop of wire pokes out of the satchel.

The board buzzes. Light after light. The sheriff's line clicks live.

SHERIFF DUNMORE (V.O.)
Millbrook exchange. Dunmore. Who's there with you, Miss Przybylski?

Mabel's hand rests on the key. She looks at Crowe. Crowe looks at the door.

MABEL
Stay where you are, Mr. Crowe. Toby, lock that door.

TOBY
Yes, ma'am.

The bolt slides home. The lamp flickers. The board keeps buzzing.
